.TH FREXP 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
frexp, frexpf, frexpl, ldexp, ldexpf, ldexpl \- convert floating-point number to fractional and integral components
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double frexp(double " x ", int *" exp );
.br
.BI "float frexpf(float " x ", int *" exp );
.br
.BI "long double frexpl(long double " x ", int *" exp );
.PP
.BI "double ldexp(double " x ", int " exp );
.br
.BI "float ldexpf(float " x ", int " exp );
.br
.BI "long double ldexpl(long double " x ", int " exp );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR frexp ()
function decomposes its first argument
.I x
into a normalized fraction and an integral power of two.  It returns
the fraction
.I frac
such that the absolute value of
.I frac
lies in the half-open interval
.BR [0.5,\ 1.0) ,
and stores the exponent through the pointer
.IR exp ,
so that the original value satisfies
.PP
.RS 4
.IR x " == " frac " * 2^" *exp .
.RE
.PP
The
.BR ldexp ()
function performs the inverse operation: it returns
.PP
.RS 4
.IR x " * 2^" exp ,
.RE
.PP
that is, the value of
.I x
multiplied by 2 raised to the power
.IR exp .
.PP
The
.BR frexpf ()
and
.BR ldexpf ()
variants take and return
.B float
values; the
.BR frexpl ()
and
.BR ldexpl ()
variants take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR frexp ()
returns the fractional part of
.IR x ,
with the magnitude in
.BR [0.5,\ 1.0) ,
and stores the corresponding exponent in
.RI * exp .
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 (\-0),
.BR frexp ()
returns +0 (\-0) and stores 0 in
.RI * exp .
.PP
If
.I x
is positive infinity (negative infinity),
.BR frexp ()
returns positive infinity (negative infinity); the value stored in
.RI * exp
is unspecified.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN,
.BR frexp ()
returns a NaN; the value stored in
.RI * exp
is unspecified.
.PP
On success,
.BR ldexp ()
returns the value
.IR x " * 2^" exp .
.PP
If the result of
.BR ldexp ()
overflows the range of the return type, a range (overflow) error
occurs and the function returns
.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
(or
.B \(+-HUGE_VALF
or
.BR \(+-HUGE_VALL )
with the sign of
.IR x .
.PP
If the result of
.BR ldexp ()
underflows the range of the return type, a range (underflow) error
occurs and the function returns 0 with the sign of
.IR x .
.PP
If
.I x
is \(+-0, \(+-infinity, or NaN, or if
.I exp
is 0,
.BR ldexp ()
returns
.I x
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur for
.BR ldexp ()
and its variants:
.TP
Range error: result overflow
An overflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.TP
Range error: result underflow
An underflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_UNDERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.PP
.BR frexp ()
and its variants do not raise floating-point exceptions and do not set
.IR errno .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR frexp (),
.BR frexpf (),
.BR frexpl (),
.BR ldexp (),
.BR ldexpf (),
.BR ldexpl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR frexp (),
.BR ldexp (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments follows C99 Annex F.10.3 (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program demonstrates a
.BR frexp ()
followed by
.BR ldexp ()
roundtrip, recovering the original value from its fractional and
exponent components:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = 1234.5;
    int exp;
    double frac, y;

    frac = frexp(x, &exp);
    y = ldexp(frac, exp);

    printf("x    = %.6f\en", x);
    printf("frac = %.6f, exp = %d\en", frac, exp);
    printf("y    = %.6f\en", y);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
x    = 1234.500000
frac = 0.602783, exp = 11
y    = 1234.500000
.EE
.RE
